  "problem": "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{\u2033}-3t{y}^{\u2032}+3y=0$ for $t>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{t}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032} & ={u}^{\u2032}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033} & ={u}^{\u2033}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{\u2032}{t}^{2}+6ut.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{\u2033}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{\u2032}{t}^{2}+6ut)-3t({u}^{\u2032}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2})+3u{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{\u2033}{t}^{2}+6{u}^{\u2032}t+6u-3{u}^{\u2032}t-9u+3u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{\u2033}{t}^{2}+3{u}^{\u2032}t) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4}({u}^{\u2033}t+3{u}^{\u2032}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{\u2033}+3{u}^{\u2032}=0.\n$$",
